#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =2j+t[i-2j] \\\\  & =t\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\n\ty = 2-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(1-y)^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(1-(2-2t))^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-1)^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-2j) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\cdot (1)+(t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-6t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}-6t+1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}-3+1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e28c9e-2228-50d4-b676-f6484c1b9e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}e^{3 - 2t} + \\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177730} {:answer/id #uuid "10691e6c-af5a-594a-b543-d69d9c8da3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}e^{2 + 3t} + \\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177731} {:answer/id #uuid "2050ccfb-94cc-5c91-b0d9-4c7a9da71943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}e^{-2t} + \\ln (3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177732} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc7f10b-b993-57a8-9231-98c8d45d434a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}3e^{2 + 3t} - 2\\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177733} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff8c510-3cf3-51a4-8eff-d968f9f33374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} - 2e^{3 - 2t} + 3\\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177734}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a261db06-f27f-544f-817f-62483c77b794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177729} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b081a3f8-5d8f-5f9a-99c1-6db960fad9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387569} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb1ba5a-5c13-5cea-85f4-897235a023e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2+3t}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387570} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2f8ba-dc02-5934-b9c4-730a95923e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387571} {:answer/id #uuid "cf295d2c-d5ca-5154-8827-1597a93d6e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3-2t}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387572} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d48b2e-8bb9-5e98-bb9f-2d4fb900164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{2+3t}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b081a3f8-5d8f-5f9a-99c1-6db960fad9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387569} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde08ab3-6529-5baf-9022-6a7982378821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88b31475-15e6-5485-a3fc-dfd5113d0f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-124701" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3,2)$ to the point $B(1,5)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i+2\\,j$ and $b=i+5\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t[(i+5\\,j)-(3\\,i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t(-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3-2t,\\,y=2+3t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\cdot (-2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f27ef8e-0147-52c1-a878-3bfc73978cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414839} {:answer/id #uuid "4b526b24-81f5-50f4-b735-c24b3e77e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414849} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0e0ea2-e6aa-590e-84ad-cadfa81c2549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414858} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd06a3d-39cb-5a6e-a180-c17cff148c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414865} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbf77d8-aea4-5c04-bc8a-1b07a1866351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd06a3d-39cb-5a6e-a180-c17cff148c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414865} :answer-field/id #uuid "243882e8-5b20-5cd8-9637-d6f35b444dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4e0663c-1824-56f9-b923-60eb7cd3a338" :question/math-academy-id "q-124703" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(2,7)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-1)j \\\\  & =i+4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5390cbb6-e9c6-59e4-9a5b-24151116d728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257498} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4d9da5-2444-52e4-93e7-11c321de4294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257595} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b06c5f-5030-5d6e-9a2e-ccefc77a4522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258512} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfd7f95-3f03-5bc4-a78a-566ae88c1873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258830} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4d7b10-9f72-5016-b9b1-72a012738ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{176}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d4d9da5-2444-52e4-93e7-11c321de4294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257595} :answer-field/id #uuid "137eadde-48c1-5096-add7-e5d9caf24988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "150b42f1-7628-5a99-bfed-f0542c112a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-124706" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve ${y}^{3}=x+3$ traversed from the point $A(-3,0)$ to the point $B(5,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=x+3\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{3}-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{3}-3))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-3)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{5}-9{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{5}-8{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{t}^{5}-8{t}^{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}-\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{6}}{2}-\\frac{8(2)^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =32-\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bbaf74-ca3d-559c-8465-6966c6b2627a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256987} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2905a2-0493-53cb-9bfa-7cebff76181e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257430} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc71395-3cbb-5ba2-ad1d-cf88d874be5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258019} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7af3ac-be89-5c73-97fd-38de59ab7d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258709} {:answer/id #uuid "e4311166-ae91-56cd-ab7d-397b6a8afccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2905a2-0493-53cb-9bfa-7cebff76181e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257430} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d511a92-4a4e-5cc5-9bef-0c5e00f92d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68c03a79-0b0a-5308-9532-cf00ca2d646f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125027" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+1)j \\\\  & =i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =t+18{t}^{3}+6t \\\\  & =7t+18{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7t+18{t}^{3})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9(1)^{4}}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d81859-b818-5dab-8b5b-0a4ad9b37a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}+{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414841} {:answer/id #uuid "7e023ba3-cbaf-5bdd-8624-aebf72479eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-3)^{2}+{e}^{t-1}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414867} {:answer/id #uuid "81fc43fd-7ab5-5169-91d7-e110be9b07b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414870} {:answer/id #uuid "8341fafe-480b-544d-a970-50af229d306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414871} {:answer/id #uuid "c83e8152-70e7-5e4f-8560-989d3d076b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}2(t-3)^{2}-3{e}^{5t-1}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8341fafe-480b-544d-a970-50af229d306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414871} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f913e6a-566c-57ec-ae58-510ebea86214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6205682e-6a34-5394-bdf6-22819f2b4d42" :question/math-academy-id "q-125044" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-2,1)$ to the point $B(1,-1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-2\\,i+j$ and $b=i-j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t(3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-2+3t,\\,y=1-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & =(-2+3t+1-2t)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2+3t-(1-2t)}\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-2}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3+{e}^{5t-3}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0896e9b5-ca4e-5f0b-9004-d268fdf6d4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256856} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0bfe57-225b-5522-a788-54741dd8ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+6\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257175} {:answer/id #uuid "4fae8de5-cd0e-5297-bd38-26f4c3589338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t)+6\\sqrt{6}t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257464} {:answer/id #uuid "9695fdc8-d9ef-557e-928a-c50f9e201736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+2\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258112} {:answer/id #uuid "a30bdc60-9342-5125-a388-c1975d8fde4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t)+3\\sqrt{6}t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9695fdc8-d9ef-557e-928a-c50f9e201736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+2\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258112} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1fbf46-6ab5-5b8e-b9ac-fb7e9724a384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1e4da74-c912-5fe5-936d-1666e265c014" :question/math-academy-id "q-125047" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+\\sqrt{xy}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(4,3)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(1+3t)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=i+j$ and $b=4\\,i+3\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[(4\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(3i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1+3t,\\,y=1+2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+\\sqrt{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+3t+1+2t)\\,i+\\sqrt{(1+3t)(1+2t)}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)\\cdot 3+\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+2\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+5t)+2\\sqrt{1+5t+6{t}^{2}}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e1ac29-28b7-50ae-9805-d51135e77c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(3t)^{7}+{5}^{4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257478} {:answer/id #uuid "7f05109c-2d3f-528e-9d6b-be6c3d65c4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257915} {:answer/id #uuid "a752374c-74bb-5865-a2ef-79db87113ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(5+4t)^{7}+3\\cdot {5}^{2+3t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258241} {:answer/id #uuid "c4024dcc-5604-56f1-ae54-7ccf671c35c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(2+3t)^{7}+4\\cdot {5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258498} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c06120-c14e-532f-9b84-dfba218f82c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c06120-c14e-532f-9b84-dfba218f82c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258668} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aefe4f7-7f96-589c-8f5a-f23e7da1cb5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73756350-0217-5bec-bce7-083a72fddb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-125048" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2{x}^{7}\\,i+{5}^{x+y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,3)$ to the point $B(5,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(2+3t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i+3\\,j$ and $b=5\\,i+4\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+3\\,j)+t[(5\\,i+4\\,j)-(2\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i+3\\,j)+t(3\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2+3t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2+3t,\\,y=3+t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2{x}^{7}\\,i+{5}^{x+y}\\,j \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{2+3t+3+t}\\,j \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{5+4t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{5+4t}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i+j) \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\cdot 3+{5}^{5+4t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d819fe-9956-59d5-8b89-01b55aa1b1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257072} {:answer/id #uuid "549f0c41-202e-53a3-8133-5585f5bd525f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257504} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a654c3-b094-5d08-b1ab-1cd5eb6c7148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258219} {:answer/id #uuid "b6817b67-cdb1-5342-b794-3be832e59dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258368} {:answer/id #uuid "cc17be62-2b53-5f4d-8d64-4379ca34cbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4a654c3-b094-5d08-b1ab-1cd5eb6c7148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258219} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fe7e29-2bae-5992-a703-7a5a5b07cc37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70644f1f-e367-534c-b7b4-edf3fcb122f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125053" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t[(4\\,i+2\\,j)-(2\\,i)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2t+2\\,y=2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot 2+{e}^{2t+2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2})\\,dt \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2}){e}^{2t}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2})({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0719b84d-826a-57c8-83fd-7d7f4d672662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256842} {:answer/id #uuid "15d65f21-dd6d-589b-8b9c-2ad70fcb3832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256976} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3575ec-991b-5520-9c8f-e84b76adfbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257568} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7554b3-be8b-5d81-9cc7-75897c2a720c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257599} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a8783b-0bfd-5216-bf0e-0e869c078593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3575ec-991b-5520-9c8f-e84b76adfbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e06902-20f2-5350-9460-18f9f5e02942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "184f4871-87e0-5c99-aba0-b9fc9a5e102d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125065" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x=0$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡t}{2}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}+{e}^{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080e7cd8-d7de-59da-9d76-4c3926fe8d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t+6\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256849} {:answer/id #uuid "0edc6431-90f7-5e1d-b499-f7ae3ae1a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256924} {:answer/id #uuid "71f3670f-e5ef-5da2-a918-90f0d4b6d0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-5\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257782} {:answer/id #uuid "c113db9b-57b5-5bf6-8607-6b5db9826594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-4\\sqrt{t}+3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258469} {:answer/id #uuid "f47f4b65-3efd-5a6e-ac29-5199cda9a475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t+5\\sqrt{t}+3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f3670f-e5ef-5da2-a918-90f0d4b6d0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-5\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257782} :answer-field/id #uuid "db579886-8ac5-5bd3-8425-185ab1a6bf1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a586e9b0-863c-55ae-99d8-d2f2e26693ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-125119" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2y\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\sqrt{y}=x+3$ traversed from the point $A(-3,0)$ to the point $B(-1,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(\\sqrt{t}-3)\\,i+t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\sqrt{t}-3)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t}-3,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2t\\,i+(\\sqrt{t}-3)^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(t-6\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2t\\,i+(t-6\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+t-6\\sqrt{t}+9 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t-6\\sqrt{t}+9 \\\\  & =t-5\\sqrt{t}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-5\\sqrt{t}+9)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a6c9e24-14ad-51e9-aa45-4d1df60cc434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257570} {:answer/id #uuid "75e6f1a7-453f-5d9c-979e-9c68f72cd9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257816} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd66e63-8593-5216-901b-36a7d75c452b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258031} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bbf585-2538-5514-a04c-348007612871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258327} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bdb68d-5be4-59b5-8948-72c54706c942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bdb68d-5be4-59b5-8948-72c54706c942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258504} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01a598b-b83f-5863-8bdb-0dee44f45e9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c359f485-3a3d-5def-a8c7-a71d4c1cd8eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125170" :question/problem "![](images/00/00ff3216e6876673a388eb3eaa0e7d02e268f5e679593ebd9fe01e2840099e1b.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+xy\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130d8ab0-b832-5552-8a82-0f5b72af4df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387618} {:answer/id #uuid "af7d44d0-10ee-5099-bc0d-5d1783ff5d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387619} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfa0109-20d8-55f6-a2d1-e087408ea821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387620} {:answer/id #uuid "10be3ee5-2152-53b4-aeb7-cb1e0f67201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387621} {:answer/id #uuid "48760dcb-b235-5f3b-abc9-de9f54794951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130d8ab0-b832-5552-8a82-0f5b72af4df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387618} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5af05b2-7703-5f0b-93c0-7a366126b21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "650eb0a9-0597-5e63-9ed1-143928ee022a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125171" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7bacafcbe893c0d21ea45a916190a49aa3a3532834cff4704f707c100bf8e6a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=3y\\,i+2x\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(0,3)$ to $B(-1,0)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the parametrization of an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively. It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =3y\\,i+2x\\,j \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e28cc7-f7f3-51e1-bea0-158eef714f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414829} {:answer/id #uuid "08339a10-b80f-5555-8e33-e4cd3d9e86b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414831} {:answer/id #uuid "1263d5b8-2a16-5cf4-bfba-339ea29a3a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414837} {:answer/id #uuid "5773a110-68a4-55f1-abfc-418c6517f35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414851} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6ccc95-f5af-538e-adc2-b6c8fdfcacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b6ccc95-f5af-538e-adc2-b6c8fdfcacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414864} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60002fd-79c9-55af-8350-d15ad8aa640e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17a6c1da-e7e7-5509-a937-e43376741415" :question/math-academy-id "q-125234" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+27{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{[{sin}^{3}⁡t-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{2}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =9[0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ffa8da-77e2-5ad2-bae3-6db12b358938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414848} {:answer/id #uuid "61d9375e-62b8-5dc5-b103-2e95fb008d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414855} {:answer/id #uuid "89b35800-42e0-534e-9730-7f543a8d03ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414874} {:answer/id #uuid "973d4a62-26aa-5079-a393-3d73fa6f1bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414878} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a88e5c-5edd-53db-b49c-ff476f854690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "973d4a62-26aa-5079-a393-3d73fa6f1bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414878} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e03b21-80ec-5be8-9b81-321307ca2b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e19e4fc2-d0da-5e51-a13e-b5b2b6e03a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-39341" :question/problem "![](images/48/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c7893f-0b53-5740-8fbd-ebe54258cfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324273} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8031ce-b980-5eb0-9766-6549305fa866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324768} {:answer/id #uuid "95bc9fcf-3ac1-5ef9-8027-af87ed0205b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325248} {:answer/id #uuid "b23599ac-9233-5e96-a595-f5c52a1d949f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325498} {:answer/id #uuid "f81eed55-5dd8-5481-8cb2-750b01e06593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b23599ac-9233-5e96-a595-f5c52a1d949f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325498} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8011e5-0bf7-5c27-90ed-97013e5119ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4c5cd42-1163-5d1e-b9ef-190caf4e41ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-39427" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e026312ab8a2ccfc61a37246c954b56550316661f158743cc2c46411a97f53.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2$  traversed counterclockwise from the point $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Notice that we can write its equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our curve is a part-ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$ respectively. This curve can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
